# ---------- G. EVIDENCE, HASH, PDF & LEGAL ----------
("G","What does the SHA-256 value prove?","Legal expert","Medium",
 "That the stored text has not changed since the digest was computed. It is a SHA-256 over the UTF-8 bytes of the raw text only, so it detects edits to that text. It does not cover the channel name, timestamp or any screenshot.",
 "Dossier: 'SHA-256 integrity hash' and its scope line 'UTF-8 rawText bytes'.",
 "So the whole record is protected?","No. Only the raw text is hashed."),
("G","Does it prove the post is authentic or from that channel?","Legal expert","Hard",
 "No. A digest says nothing about where the text came from. For Telegram preview scans the source type and the source timestamp are recorded, but there is no signed capture or screenshot.",
 "Source label line under a post.",
 "Then what is the digest for?","Detecting later changes and referring to an exact version of the text."),
("G","Is the evidence admissible in court?","Legal expert","Hard",
 "I am not a lawyer. As far as I know, electronic records need a certificate from the person in charge of the device or system under the evidence law (formerly Section 65B of the Evidence Act, now Section 63 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam). PRAHARI does not issue that certificate; it prepares a record that supports one.",
 "PDF certificate box: 'SHA-256 content digest, not a legal certificate'.",
 "Your older slide said court-ready.","That wording was too strong and was removed. The prototype is evidence-supporting, not court-certified."),
